<!--
 Copyright (C) 2026 the webauth-portal authors

 SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-or-later

 INCUBATING PUBLIC PORTAL INTERFACE, version 1.

 This is the interface APPLICATIONS call. It is a project-controlled reverse-DNS
 name and is deliberately NOT in the org.freedesktop.portal.* namespace: that
 namespace belongs to xdg-desktop-portal, and shipping a name from it would assert
 an acceptance that does not exist.

 The SHAPE, however, is xdg-desktop-portal's, deliberately and exactly:

   io.github.sjtrotter.portal.WebAuthentication1   <-> org.freedesktop.portal.<Name>
   io.github.sjtrotter.portal.Desktop (bus name)   <-> org.freedesktop.portal.Desktop
   /io/github/sjtrotter/portal/desktop             <-> /org/freedesktop/portal/desktop
   io.github.sjtrotter.portal.Request              <-> org.freedesktop.portal.Request

 Modelled on org.freedesktop.portal.Account and org.freedesktop.portal.FileChooser:
 https://github.com/flatpak/xdg-desktop-portal/blob/main/data/org.freedesktop.portal.Account.xml
 https://flatpak.github.io/xdg-desktop-portal/docs/doc-org.freedesktop.portal.Request.html
 https://flatpak.github.io/xdg-desktop-portal/docs/window-identifiers.html

 The frontend that exports this interface performs NO user interaction. It derives
 the caller's app id, validates the arguments, and forwards the call to a backend
 implementing io.github.sjtrotter.impl.portal.WebAuthentication1 (see
 ../../backends/gtk/data/io.github.sjtrotter.impl.portal.WebAuthentication1.xml and
 docs/IMPL-INTERFACE.md). Applications must never call the impl interface; see
 docs/SECURITY.md.

 The eventual upstream name would be org.freedesktop.portal.WebAuthentication.
 Nothing has been proposed to anyone. See docs/UPSTREAMING.md and docs/ROADMAP.md.
-->
<node name="/" xmlns:doc="http://www.freedesktop.org/dbus/1.0/doc.dtd">

  <!--
      io.github.sjtrotter.portal.WebAuthentication1:
      @short_description: One interactive web authentication transaction

      Performs one interactive web authentication transaction and returns an
      uninterpreted completion artifact.

      The caller supplies a URI to open and the exact URI whose navigation ends
      the transaction. A backend opens the start URI in a web view it owns,
      presents security chrome the caller cannot influence, answers TLS client
      certificate challenges (including PKCS#11 tokens that require a PIN),
      watches every top level navigation, and completes the transaction on the
      first navigation whose URI matches the completion URI. The web view is
      closed before that navigation is loaded, so the completion page is never
      fetched and never rendered.

      Neither the frontend nor the backend interprets the URI that is returned.
      Neither has any knowledge of OAuth, OpenID Connect or SAML, neither
      exchanges anything, and neither stores credentials. Callers implement
      their protocol on top.

      A TLS client certificate challenge is satisfied by the backend, through an
      adapter with two implementations: the smart card portal
      (io.github.sjtrotter.portal.Smartcard1) where it is available and proven,
      and an in-process chooser and PIN prompt otherwise. Which one ran is not
      visible through this interface. When neither can run, the challenge is
      declined and the transaction ends with response 2 and reason
      "no_certificate_adapter".

      Version 1 supports navigation (GET) completions only. A response that
      arrives as an HTTP POST body - OpenID Connect response_mode=form_post, or
      SAML HTTP-POST binding - is NOT supported and cannot be represented as a
      completion URI. Supporting it would need a separately reviewed result of
      the shape { method, uri, content_type, body_fd }; squeezing a form body
      into a synthetic URI is not an acceptable substitute.

      This interface is exported on the bus name
      io.github.sjtrotter.portal.Desktop, at the object path
      /io/github/sjtrotter/portal/desktop, alongside every other interface that
      incubating frontend hosts - exactly as xdg-desktop-portal hosts every
      portal interface on org.freedesktop.portal.Desktop.

      This documentation describes version 1 of this interface.
  -->
  <interface name="io.github.sjtrotter.portal.WebAuthentication1">

    <!--
        Start:
        @parent_window: Identifier for the calling application's window, in the
          xdg-desktop-portal window identifier format ("x11:&lt;xid&gt;",
          "wayland:&lt;exported handle&gt;"). May be empty. Passed through to the
          backend uninterpreted; the backend parses it. An invalid or expired
          identifier degrades to an unparented window; it never aborts the
          transaction.
        @start_uri: The URI to open. Must be an absolute https URI with a host.
        @completion_uri: The URI whose navigation ends the transaction. Matched
          exactly on the parsed URI; see "Completion matching" in
          docs/PUBLIC-INTERFACE.md. Query and fragment carry the result and take
          no part in matching.
        @options: Vardict with optional further information
        @request_handle: Object path of the io.github.sjtrotter.portal.Request
          object for this call

        Supported keys in the @options vardict:
        <variablelist>
          <varlistentry>
            <term>handle_token s</term>
            <listitem><para>
              A caller-chosen unguessable token used as the last element of
              @request_handle, so the caller can subscribe to ::Response before
              calling Start and avoid the response race. Must be a valid object
              path element.
            </para></listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term>activation_token s</term>
            <listitem><para>
              An XDG activation token authorizing the window to take focus.
              Parenting and activation are related but not interchangeable: a
              background caller without a valid activation token does not
              silently steal focus.
            </para></listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term>session_mode s</term>
            <listitem><para>
              "shared" (default) or "ephemeral". This is a WEB SESSION STORAGE
              mode, not an io.github.sjtrotter.portal.Session object: this
              interface creates no Session, because a transaction is not a long
              lived session. "shared" means shared among this backend's
              transactions - NOT with Firefox, Chrome or the user's default
              browser. "ephemeral" runs the transaction in a website data store
              created for it and destroyed with it. A caller may request
              ephemeral; it may never defeat a policy that forces ephemeral.
              There is no per-application mode in version 1.
            </para></listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term>timeout u</term>
            <listitem><para>
              Seconds before the transaction is abandoned. A caller may only
              shorten the default of 300; the hard ceiling is 900. Smart card
              and MFA flows make short fixed timeouts hostile. The frontend
              clamps the value it forwards; the backend enforces its own
              deadline as well.
            </para></listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term>title s</term>
            <listitem><para>
              A purpose hint, shown beneath the backend's own security chrome
              and never as the window's trusted identity. It is caller-supplied
              text and is treated as such: a caller cannot label itself
              "Microsoft Login". Length-limited by the frontend.
            </para></listitem>
          </varlistentry>
        </variablelist>

        Unknown option keys are DROPPED by the frontend rather than forwarded,
        so an option can be added without a version bump and a backend never
        sees a key the frontend does not know. Unknown VALUES for a known key
        are an error: silently falling back from "ephemeral" to "shared" would
        be a security decision made by a typo.

        The result is delivered through
        io.github.sjtrotter.portal.Request::Response with these keys:
        <variablelist>
          <varlistentry>
            <term>completion_uri s</term>
            <listitem><para>
              The matched URI, complete and undecoded, exactly as the web view
              was asked to navigate to it, including query and fragment.
              Obtained from the engine rather than reconstructed. Present only
              when @response is 0. The frontend re-checks this value against the
              @completion_uri it forwarded before emitting it; see
              docs/IMPL-INTERFACE.md.
            </para></listitem>
          </varlistentry>
          <varlistentry>
            <term>reason s</term>
            <listitem><para>
              Optional stable symbol explaining a non-zero @response. A
              diagnostic only.
            </para></listitem>
          </varlistentry>
        </variablelist>

        @response values follow the portal convention:
        0 the transaction completed and @results carries "completion_uri",
        1 the user cancelled, or the caller called Close(),
        2 the transaction ended some other way - timeout expired, no backend
        available, no web engine, no display, malformed request, the backend
        terminated, the backend returned a completion URI that is not the one
        that was requested, or a client certificate was required and no
        certificate adapter could run.

        A response of 1 or 2 may carry an optional "reason s" key: a stable
        symbol such as "timeout", "no_display", "no_backend",
        "backend_disappeared", "backend_completion_mismatch" or
        "no_certificate_adapter". It is a diagnostic, never a substitute for the
        response code, and a consumer must tolerate its absence.
    -->
    <method name="Start">
      <arg type="s" name="parent_window" direction="in"/>
      <arg type="s" name="start_uri" direction="in"/>
      <arg type="s" name="completion_uri" direction="in"/>
      <annotation name="org.qtproject.QtDBus.QtTypeName.In3" value="QVariantMap"/>
      <arg type="a{sv}" name="options" direction="in"/>
      <arg type="o" name="request_handle" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!--
        version:

        The version of this interface that is implemented. 1.
    -->
    <property name="version" type="u" access="read"/>
  </interface>

  <!--
      io.github.sjtrotter.portal.Request:
      @short_description: A pending portal request

      The same shape and the same semantics as org.freedesktop.portal.Request,
      under this project's own name, and shared by every interface this
      incubating frontend hosts. There is deliberately no separate Cancel
      method: Close() is the cancellation mechanism, exactly as in the portal
      pattern.

      Object path:
      /io/github/sjtrotter/portal/desktop/request/&lt;SENDER&gt;/&lt;TOKEN&gt;
      where &lt;SENDER&gt; is the caller's unique name with the initial ":"
      removed and every "." replaced by "_", and &lt;TOKEN&gt; is the caller's
      handle_token - the convention documented at
      https://flatpak.github.io/xdg-desktop-portal/docs/doc-org.freedesktop.portal.Request.html

      Upstream ships this interface in its own file
      (data/org.freedesktop.portal.Request.xml) because every portal shares it.
      It is carried here only so this repository is self-contained; at
      acceptance this node is deleted and the existing shared interface is used
      unchanged. See docs/UPSTREAMING.md.
  -->
  <interface name="io.github.sjtrotter.portal.Request">

    <!--
        Close:

        Cancels the request. The frontend calls Close() on the backend's
        io.github.sjtrotter.impl.portal.Request object, which closes the web
        view and every dialog belonging to the transaction, and emits
        ::Response with @response 1 if it has not already responded. Close()
        never produces a later success response. Closing an already answered
        request is not an error and does nothing: a caller that times out
        locally at the moment the user finishes must not get an error and must
        not get two responses.

        A completion that has already been committed atomically wins over a
        simultaneous Close(); every other late event is discarded.
    -->
    <method name="Close"/>

    <!--
        Response:
        @response: 0 completed, 1 cancelled, 2 ended some other way
        @results: Vardict, carrying "completion_uri s" when @response is 0

        Emitted exactly once per request. After it is emitted the request object
        is unexported and the transaction is forgotten. The result is bound to
        the D-Bus connection that called Start: if that connection goes away the
        transaction is cancelled and no result is delivered to anyone else.
    -->
    <signal name="Response">
      <arg type="u" name="response"/>
      <annotation name="org.qtproject.QtDBus.QtTypeName.Out1" value="QVariantMap"/>
      <arg type="a{sv}" name="results"/>
    </signal>
  </interface>
</node>
